localHilbertScheme -- computes a power series representation of the local Hilbert scheme
****************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        (F,R,G,C) = localHilbertScheme(F0)
  * Inputs:
      * F0, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * CacheName => ..., default value null
      * CorrectionMatrix => ..., default value "auto"
      * DefParam => ..., default value "t"
      * *note DegreeBound: DegreeBound, => ..., default value 0, determines the
        degree limit used to compute the tangent cone of obstruction equations
      * HighestOrder => ..., default value 20
      * PolynomialCheck => ..., default value true
      * SanityCheck => ..., default value true
      * SmartLift => ..., default value true
      * *note Verbose: Verbose, => ..., default value 0, control the verbosity
        of output
  * Outputs:
      * F, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List, of matrices
      * R, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List, of matrices
      * G, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List, of matrices
      * C, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List, of matrices

Description
===========

F0 should  be a matrix with homogeneous entries over some polynomial ring with
one row.

Each element of the sequence (F,R,G,C) is a list of matrices in increasing
powers of the deformation parameter specified by *note DefParam: DefParam,.
Their sums satisfy the deformation equation transpose ((sum F)*(sum R))+(sum
C)*(sum G)==0 up to powers of the deformation parameter equal to the length of
F. Furthermore, F_0=F0, R_0=gens ker F0, C_0=T^2(0,F_0) and F_1 consists of
first order perturbations corresponding to normalMatrix(0,F0). Thus, F and G
represent a universal family and local analytic equations for the Hilbert
scheme.

Several options are available to control the termination of the calculation.
The calculation will terminate at the very latest after reaching order equal to
the option *note HighestOrder: HighestOrder,, which has default value 20. If
this order is reached, a warning message is generated. If *note
PolynomialCheck: PolynomialCheck, is set to *note true: (Macaulay2Doc)true,, as
is the default, then the algorithm will check if the present solution lifts to
infinite order and terminate if this is the case. If *note SanityCheck:
SanityCheck, is set to *note true: (Macaulay2Doc)true,, as is the default, then
the algorithm will check that the present solution really does solve the
deformation equation, and terminate with an error if this is not the case.

The option *note Verbose: (Macaulay2Doc)Verbose, may be used to control the
verbosity of the output. Its value should be an integer, with higher values
corresponding to more verbose output. Default value is 0.

The option *note SmartLift: SmartLift, is also available, which controls
whether the algorithm spends extra time trying to find liftings which introduce
no new obstructions at the next highest order. By default, this option is
enabled. The option *note CorrectionMatrix: CorrectionMatrix, may be used to
control which liftings are considered.

After each step of lifting, the solution (F,R,G,C) to the deformation equation
is cached. By default, it is stored in F0.cache#VersalDeformationResults but
may stored elsewhere by setting the option *note CacheName: CacheName, to
something other than *note null: (Macaulay2Doc)null,.

For example, consider a degenerate twisted cubic curve, see *note [PS85]: Top,:

+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : S=QQ[x,y,z,w];                                       |
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : F0=matrix {{x*z,y*z,z^2,x^3}}                        |
|                                                          |
|o2 = | xz yz z2 x3 |                                      |
|                                                          |
|             1      4                                     |
|o2 : Matrix S  <-- S                                      |
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : (F,R,G,C)=localHilbertScheme(F0,Verbose=>2);         |
|Calculating first order deformations and obstruction space|
|Calculating first order relations                         |
|Calculating standard expressions for obstructions         |
|Starting lifting                                          |
|Order 2                                                   |
|Order 3                                                   |
|Order 4                                                   |
|Order 5                                                   |
|Order 6                                                   |
|Order 7                                                   |
|Solution is polynomial                                    |
+----------------------------------------------------------+

Local equations for the Hilbert scheme are thus given by

+----------------------------+
|i4 : T=ring first G;        |
+----------------------------+
|i5 : sum G                  |
|                            |
|o5 = | t_1t_16             ||
|     | t_9t_16             ||
|     | -t_4t_16            ||
|     | -2t_14t_16+t_15t_16 ||
|                            |
|             4      1       |
|o5 : Matrix T  <-- T        |
+----------------------------+

Caveat
======

The output may not be the local Hilbert scheme if standard comparison theorems
do not hold for the ideal generated by FO. This may be tested using *note
checkComparisonTheorem: checkComparisonTheorem, or *note checkTangentSpace:
checkTangentSpace,.

Ways to use localHilbertScheme:
===============================

  * "localHilbertScheme(Matrix)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note localHilbertScheme: localHilbertScheme, is a *note method
function with options: (Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunctionWithOptions,.

