nestedVersalDeformation -- computes a nested VersalDeformation of a pair
************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        D = nestedVersalDeformation(F0X,F0Y)
  * Inputs:
      * F0X, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,
      * F0Y, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note DefParamX: DefParam, => ..., default value "s", deformation
        parameter
      * *note DefParamY: DefParam, => ..., default value "t", deformation
        parameter
      * *note DegreeBound: DegreeBound, => ..., default value 0, determines the
        degree limit used to compute the tangent cone of obstruction equations
      * *note HighestOrder: HighestOrder, => ..., default value 20, sets the
        order to which we compute
      * IncludeTrivial => ..., default value true
      * *note PolynomialCheck: PolynomialCheck, => ..., default value true,
        checks if power series solution terminates
      * Projective => ..., default value false
      * *note SanityCheck: SanityCheck, => ..., default value true, checks if
        lifting solves deformation equation
      * *note Verbose: Verbose, => ..., default value 0, control the verbosity
        of output
  * Outputs:
      * D, an instance of the type *note NestedDeformation: NestedDeformation,

Description
===========

F0X and F0Y are matrices over a common ring and respectively encode equations a
subscheme/quotient module and its ambient scheme/module.

If *note SanityCheck: SanityCheck, is set to *note true: (Macaulay2Doc)true,,
as is the default, then the algorithm will check that the relevant deformation
equations are satisfied, and terminate with an error if this is not the case.

The parameters used in the perturbations may be specified by the options *note
DefParamX: DefParam, and *note DefParamY: DefParam,.

If *note Projective: (Macaulay2Doc)Projective, is set to *note true:
(Macaulay2Doc)true, then only degree zero deformations are considered.

For example, consider the cone over the rational normal curve of degree four
and a fat point at the origin cut out by the square of the homogeneous maximal
ideal. The following computes the versal deformation of this pair:

